\title{PRSTR: plan to increase emphasis on ``model.matrix'' and incorporate ``Regression and other stories''.\\
  Two meanings of ``contrast'' in R:\\
  coding the model versus specifying the comparisons, limma's makeContrasth, glht and mcp's contrMat, emmeans, model.matrix, options(contrasts), et al.}
\author{Ramon Diaz-Uriarte, with a response by Claude}
\date{17 November 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

%% ===================================================================
\section{Ramon writes}

\paragraph{b)} Hay algo potentially confussing.

\paragraph{b.1)} La función ``contrasts'', en R, con valores como ``contr.treatment'' o ``contr.sum'' especifica cómo parametrizamos el modelo; o sea, cómo se construye la model.matrix. Interesante que el cambiar la patemetrización (el uso de \verb|options(contrasts = ...)|, o el mirar el setting vía \verb|options()$contrasts|) no aparece ni una sola vez en el documento de Law. Este uso de la función ``contrasts'' es el estándard y widespread, en R, y en la literatura as far as I remember (McCullagh \& Nelder, ``Generalized Linear Models'').

\paragraph{b.2)} Beyond that, we are interested in comparing (contrasting, testing) specific things. For example, something like ``all the experimental treatments vs.\ the Control'' or ``testing whether all pairs of means are equal'', or ``testing whether the `WT' is the average of `GoodMutation' and `BadMutation'\,''. Note that there is a possible ambiguity of ``treatment'' here, but here treatment are the labels in our factors.

The function makeContrasts in limma is used for this purpose you say ``I want to compare `WT' against the average of `GoodMutation' and `BadMutation'\,'' or ``Compare all treatments against the Control''. This returns the set of linear functions of the parameters so that you test what you want to test.

This is also what the function contrMat constructs in package multcomp. In fact, using Tukey contrasts in the multcomp package does exactly this: return the set of linear functions of the parameters so that you test what you want to test.

\paragraph{b.2)} Regardless of the setting of \verb|options()$contrasts| (e.g., whether ``contrasts.sum'' or ``contrasts.treatment'', or, if using package car, ``contrasts.Sum'', or ``contrasts.Treatment'', etc), you can always specify the comparisons you are interested in as a linear function of the parameters (the things shown under ``coefficients'') of your model. In other words, as linear functions of what is given by ``model.matrix''.

makeContrasts, in limma, makes this more transparent if you want, and you see directly what exactly you are comparing as a function of the model matrix.

contrMat in a sense hides it from you a little bit more (and, at the same time, seems more flexible).

Package emmeans uses yet another way of going around this in the sense that the function names and how one builds comparisons (aka contrasts or sets of hypothesis to test) of interest is somewhat different. (Explained in \url{https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/emmeans/vignettes/comparisons.html}) but the ideas are, of course, the same.

\paragraph{b.3)} Interestingly, the Law et al document does not discuss the settings of \verb|options()$contrasts|. The book by Dunn \& Smyth has a very brief section. And the documentation of contrMat does not dwell into it, and that of emmeans discusses it only a little.

It seems that, in particular for limma and makeContrasts, the implicit modus operandi of the authors is: `we will not mess around with changing \verb|options()$contrasts|; i.e., we won't do things like \verb|opt <- options(contrasts = c("contr.sum", "contr.poly"))|'. We just take things as R does them, and directly use the following route instead:

\begin{description}[leftmargin=3em,style=nextline]
\item[b.3.1:] we either use or not use an intercept (when, why, etc, is explained in the Law et al paper)
\item[b.3.2:] we use makeContrasts to compare what we care about
\end{description}

This is also what, to a large extent, ghlt does, and what ``Anova'' from the car package do.

In particular limma seems to say: R will parameterize the model in some way and we are just happy with it, and the only knob we turn is adding, or not, an intercept. That gives a particular model.matrix. Then, use makeContrasts.

I think the above is a reasonable summary of what is done and the sources of confusion. It would be good that it was explained.

\bigskip
\noindent\rule{\linewidth}{0.4pt}

I am still trying to figure out what ROS does here exactly. They use ``model.matrix'' exactly once in the book and \verb|options()$contrasts| (and realted) are not called even once. I need to look into that with more detail later. I think their logic is very different because, well, they are not asking for ``tests of hypothesis'' (no, no, we are Bayesians) but will eventually ask for posteriors of the things one cares about.

%% ===================================================================
\section{Claude responds and complements as}

\subsection*{(a) Why stop the reading of Law et al.\ at ``Study of interactions and additivity of treatments''}

Two reasons, one good and one accidental.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{The good one:} through that section the paper covers what session~2 and the first hour of session~3 teach (one factor, contrasts, two factors with and without interaction). After it come nested factors, time courses, mixed effects and polynomial or cyclical covariates, which go beyond the block.
\item \textbf{The accidental one:} my read of the page was cut off shortly after, so I recommended within what I had seen.
\end{itemize}

I would now extend it by one subsection, ``Accounting for factors that are not of interest'' (batch effects), since \code{\textasciitilde{} batch + condition} is the design students will meet most. I have added it as optional.

In the part of the paper I could read, \code{options(contrasts = ...)}, \code{contr.sum}, \code{contr.treatment} and \code{contrasts()} are never named; the only knobs discussed are the default alphanumeric order of factor levels and \code{relevel} to change the reference level.

\subsection*{(b) The summary is right; two refinements}

The confusion is that R uses ``contrast'' for two different objects, and b.1 and b.2 separate them correctly. Here is a way to state it that I think resolves it for students.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{The scientific question lives on the group means.} ``WT versus the average of the two mutants'' is a vector $\mathbf{c}$ applied to the vector of group (cell) means $\bm{\mu}$. It does not depend on any coding.
\item \textbf{The coding decides the model matrix.} \code{contr.treatment}, \code{contr.sum}, or dropping the intercept each give a different \code{model.matrix}, hence different coefficients $\bm{\beta}$ with different meanings, but the same fitted group means.
\item \textbf{The same question is a different vector under each coding.} Let $L$ be the matrix with one row of the model matrix per group, so that $\bm{\mu} = L\bm{\beta}$. Then
  \[
    \mathbf{c}^{\top}\bm{\mu} = (\mathbf{c}^{\top} L)\,\bm{\beta} = \mathbf{k}^{\top}\bm{\beta}.
  \]
  Change the coding and $L$, $\bm{\beta}$ and $\mathbf{k}$ change; the estimate of $\mathbf{c}^{\top}\bm{\mu}$, its standard error and its test do not.
\end{enumerate}

The tools differ only in which of these vectors you type:

\medskip
\noindent
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}>{\raggedright}p{0.30\linewidth}
    >{\raggedright}X >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.27\linewidth}@{}}
  \toprule
  Tool & What you specify & Depends on the coding? \\
  \midrule
  \code{makeContrasts} (limma)
    & $\mathbf{k}$, in terms of the design matrix's column names
    & Yes; hence limma's preference for \code{\textasciitilde{} 0 + group} \\
  \code{contrMat} and \code{glht(..., mcp())} (multcomp)
    & $\mathbf{c}$, on the factor's level means; \code{mcp()} translates
      it to $\mathbf{k}$
    & No; the translation is done for you \\
  emmeans
    & $\mathbf{c}$, on estimated marginal means
    & No \\
  ROS (\code{posterior\_epred} on a grid of cells)
    & $\mathbf{c}$, applied to each posterior draw of the cell means
    & No \\
  ROS (coefficient draws, \code{as.matrix(fit)})
    & $\mathbf{k}$, written out by hand on the coefficients
    & Yes \\
  By hand
    & $\mathbf{k}$, on \code{coef(fit)}
    & Yes \\
  \bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\medskip

The refinements:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{\code{contrMat} returns $\mathbf{c}$, not $\mathbf{k}$.} It builds contrasts among level means (Tukey, Dunnett). It is \code{glht} with \code{mcp()} that turns them into linear functions of the model's coefficients, using the model's own coding. So they become linear functions of the parameters only after that step.
\item \textbf{\code{car::Anova} is not quite in the same family.} Type~II tests are invariant to the coding. Type~III tests are not: they only mean what people expect under sum-to-zero coding. That is the one place in the course where \code{options(contrasts)} changes an answer, and it is already in \S20.6.1 and Appendix~B.3 of the notes.
\end{itemize}

The reading of limma in b.3 matches what I know: leave R's default coding alone, choose intercept or no intercept, then \code{makeContrasts}. The paper's own stated reason is that in a means model each parameter is a group mean, which makes comparisons simple to write as contrasts, while for a single treatment-versus-control comparison the mean-reference model is more direct, since its parameters already are the differences. One addition: \code{\textasciitilde{} 0 + a + b} only gives full indicator columns for the first factor, which is why the paper merges two factors into one before using a means model.

\subsection*{What ROS does (checked against the text)}

This section replaces my earlier guess. I searched the full text of the PDF released on 18 May 2025 (546 pages) and read the relevant sections. Page numbers below are the book's printed page numbers; in that PDF, the PDF page is the printed page plus 14.

\paragraph{What is absent.} \code{model.matrix} appears once, on p.~429 (\S21.2): it is used to get the predictor matrix and swap one column in order to correct the standard error of a two-stage instrumental-variables estimate. It is never used to inspect or explain how a factor is coded. \code{options(contrasts)}, \code{contr.treatment}, \code{contr.sum}, \code{contrasts()} and \code{relevel} never appear. No formula drops the intercept (\code{\textasciitilde{} 0 + g} or \code{\textasciitilde{} g - 1}). The word ``contrast'' appears 29 times, always in its everyday sense (``in contrast''), never in either of the two technical senses of part (b). Tukey or Dunnett families of comparisons do not appear either; ``multiple comparisons'' is mentioned only as one of the ways to obtain spurious statistical significance (pp.~61 and~66).

\paragraph{How factors are coded.} Always with R's default treatment coding, which the book calls the ``baseline'' (occasionally ``reference'' or ``default'') category, and always explained through indicator variables.
\begin{itemize}
\item \S7.3 (pp.~99--101) shows, by fake-data simulation, that regressing on a constant gives the mean and regressing on a 0/1 indicator gives the difference in means.
\item \S10.4 (pp.~136--139) fits \code{factor(ethnicity)} with four levels, explains that R took the alphabetically first level as baseline, and changes the baseline by rebuilding the factor with \code{factor(..., levels = c(...))}. It then walks through how every coefficient shifts by the same amount when the baseline changes, while the coefficients of the other predictors do not. This is the coding-versus-question point of part (b), shown on one example without being stated in general. It also builds the indicators by hand with \code{ifelse}, leaving one out.
\item \S10.5 (pp.~139--140) writes paired and block designs as \code{y \textasciitilde{} treatment + factor(pairs)} and notes that for the treatment effect it does not matter which block is the baseline.
\item \S12.5 (pp.~197--199) distinguishes index variables from indicator variables and explains why only $J-1$ of $J$ indicators can go in with an intercept. It mentions dropping the intercept to keep all $J$, and notes that this fails as soon as a second factor is added, so it is simpler to keep the intercept and drop one indicator per factor (the same issue as the two-factor means model in Law et al.). It lists other choices (last level as baseline, a chosen baseline, coefficients constrained to sum to zero) without showing them, and says the option the authors often prefer is a multilevel model, which is beyond the scope of the book.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{How comparisons are obtained.} Never by choosing a coding to make a coefficient equal the comparison. The book uses two routes, and both appear.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Predict the cells, then combine them} (the $\mathbf{c}$ route). \S9.2 (pp.~115--116) introduces \code{posterior\_linpred}, \code{posterior\_epred} and \code{posterior\_predict} on a \code{newdata} grid, and also computes the same draws by hand from \code{as.matrix(fit)}. The clearest instance of the $\mathbf{c}$ route is poststratification in \S17.1 (pp.~314--316): \code{stan\_glm(vote \textasciitilde{} factor(pid))}, then \code{posterior\_epred} on a data frame with one row per cell, then a matrix product of the $n_{\text{sims}}\times J$ draws with the vector of population weights. The weight vector is a $\mathbf{c}$, the grid is our $L$, and the coding never appears. With two factors and their interaction (\code{factor(pid) + male + factor(pid):male}), the book says the remaining steps are unchanged.
\item \textbf{Combine the coefficient draws by hand} (the $\mathbf{k}$ route). \S9.1--9.2 (p.~115) computes a ratio of two coefficients draw by draw. \S19.4 (pp.~370--373) computes a treatment effect that varies with pre-test as \code{sims[,2] + sims[,4]*x} for each draw, averages it over the data, and writes the population average as $\beta_1+\beta_4\mu_1+\beta_5\mu_2$, noting that its standard error is easier to get from simulations than analytically. That is $\mathbf{k}^\top\bm{\beta}$ written by hand, which depends on the coding.
\end{enumerate}
The average predictive comparisons of \S14.4 (pp.~249--252) are the same idea on the probability scale for logistic regression: predictions at two values of one input, averaged over the observed values of the others. There the book computes them from the point estimates \code{coef(fit)}, not from the draws.

\paragraph{So, for part (b).} The guess above was right about the main route and wrong to say $\mathbf{k}$ is never needed. ROS takes R's default coding as given, as limma does, but turns a different knob: limma chooses intercept or no intercept and then types $\mathbf{k}$ with \code{makeContrasts}; ROS keeps the intercept, chooses the baseline by ordering the levels, interprets coefficients as comparisons with that baseline, and for anything else either predicts the cells and applies $\mathbf{c}$ to the draws, or writes $\mathbf{k}$ by hand on the coefficient draws. ROS does not state the general point (that the question lives on the cell means and the coding only changes how it is written in coefficients); \S10.4 shows one instance of it. So, like Law et al., it will not make that point for the students; the course has to.

That still gives a clean link for session~3: the ``WT versus average of mutants'' contrast computed by hand in session~2 becomes, with \code{stan\_glm}, \code{posterior\_epred} on a three-row grid followed by \code{\%*\% c}. That is the same pattern as \S17.1, with contrast weights instead of population weights.

\subsection*{Implications for the course plan}

Session~2's second hour gets an explicit 10-minute step on the two meanings of ``contrast'', between the two parameterisations and the contrasts by hand, plus a note that neither the Law et al.\ paper nor ROS will make this point for the students.

The invariance claim is checked below with \code{lm} under three codings: treatment, sum-to-zero, and the means model. The chunk was run with R~4 (base R only). The $\mathbf{k}$ vectors differ; the estimate and its standard error are identical, and so is $\mathbf{c}$ applied to the predicted cell means, which is the ROS route without the posterior. The \code{stan\_glm} version was not run.

<<invariance>>=
## The same comparison under three codings.
set.seed(1)
d <- data.frame(y = rnorm(30),
                g = factor(rep(c("WT", "GoodMutation", "BadMutation"),
                               each = 10)))
d$g <- relevel(d$g, ref = "WT")

f_t <- lm(y ~ g, data = d)  # default coding: contr.treatment
f_s <- lm(y ~ g, data = d, contrasts = list(g = "contr.sum"))
f_0 <- lm(y ~ 0 + g, data = d)  # means model
fits <- list(treatment = f_t, sum = f_s, means = f_0)

## L: one row of the model matrix per group
nd <- data.frame(g = factor(levels(d$g), levels = levels(d$g)))
L <- function(f) model.matrix(delete.response(terms(f)), nd,
                              contrasts.arg = f$contrasts)

## c: WT versus the average of the two mutants, on the group means
cc <- c(WT = 1, GoodMutation = -0.5, BadMutation = -0.5)[levels(d$g)]

## k = c' L: different under each coding ...
ks <- lapply(fits, function(f) drop(cc %*% L(f)))
ks

## ... but the same estimate and the same standard error
sapply(names(fits), function(n) {
  k <- ks[[n]]; f <- fits[[n]]
  c(estimate = sum(k * coef(f)),
    se = sqrt(drop(t(k) %*% vcov(f) %*% k)))
})

## ROS route: predict the cells, apply c; no k needed
sapply(fits, function(f) sum(cc * predict(f, nd)))
@

<<ros-route, eval=FALSE>>=
## NOT RUN: the same comparison with rstanarm, as in ROS 17.1
library(rstanarm)
fit <- stan_glm(y ~ g, data = d, refresh = 0)
mu_draws <- posterior_epred(fit, newdata = nd)  # n_sims x 3
contrast_draws <- drop(mu_draws %*% cc)
c(median = median(contrast_draws), mad_sd = mad(contrast_draws))
@

